Proxy bypass: Node client must have NO_PROXY=*. In Node 24 with NODE_OPTIONS=--use-env-proxy, `fetch` uses env proxy? Node's built-in fetch respects `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` / `--use-env-proxy`; `NO_PROXY=*` should bypass. The task explicitly says "宿主 Node 客户端（NO_PROXY=*）". Since the script itself is run by `node scripts/...` with inherited NODE_OPTIONS=--use-env-proxy, we can set `process.env.NO_PROXY = '*'` and `process.env.no_proxy = '*'` at the top before making requests. But does Node's proxy resolution read env at request time? Likely yes (undici EnvHttpProxyAgent reads env at agent creation). To be extra safe, use `http.get` with `agent: false`? The spec says "（或 agent:false + 环境变量）". Node's core `http` module does not use env proxies by default — only `fetch` with `--use-env-proxy` does. If we use `http.get` directly, no proxy is applied, period. But the task explicitly requires NO_PROXY=*; we can set both env vars and still use `http.request` (which ignores proxies). However, to demonstrate the proxy bypass, using `fetch` with NO_PROXY=* is more aligned. But streaming with fetch is easy (response.body async iterator). Node 24's fetch is stable. Use fetch with `process.env.NO_PROXY='*'` and `no_proxy='*'`. Yet if NODE_OPTIONS=--use-env-proxy is set at process start, undici's global dispatcher may initialize on first fetch, reading env then — we set env before first fetch. Good.

Alternatively spawn client as a child process with env NO_PROXY=*? Not needed.

We need to parse SSE frames: fake serve.sh sends 5 frames `data: N\n\n` once per second, then closes. The client records arrival timestamps of each frame. Need to handle chunks — parse `\n\n` boundaries, count frames whose data starts with `data:`. Timing: record `performance.now()` at each complete frame. Alternatively record on data chunk; server writes each frame likely in separate TCP segments due to delay. For buffered path, all 5 frames arrive in one chunk, record same timestamp for all 5. Simpler and more precise: for each `data:` line, record time when line is parsed. If one chunk contains 5 frames, all timestamps nearly identical — that's fine (spread <=1s). Requirement says "记录逐帧时间". We'll parse lines, when line starts with `data:` record now. For /sse-buffered, if all frames arrive in one chunk, timestamps within a few ms.

But note nginx's buffering behavior: `proxy_buffering off` causes nginx to pass data as it arrives; default buffering means nginx buffers until buffer full/upstream close; hence 5 frames arrive when upstream closes after 5s, all at once (first frame delay ~5s). Wait, the spec's verified result: `/sse-buffered` 5 帧同一秒集中到达 (885s ×5). So first frame delay ~5s (>=4s) and all within 1s. Both conditions pass. Actually assertion says "5 帧在 <= 1s 内或首帧延迟 >= 4s". We need to define first frame delay relative to request start. With buffered, first frame at ~5s, so second condition true. Fine.

For `/sse`, adjacent interval >=500ms and total span >=3s. Since server sends 1 fps for 5 frames, span ~4s. Good. Timing variation from jitter; threshold 500ms is safe.

Important: serve.sh must read request headers before sending, to avoid RST on buffered path. Recipe from spec:
```sh
#!/bin/sh
# Read HTTP request headers line by line until blank line
while IFS= read -r line; do
  [ -z "$line" ] && break
done
printf 'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n'
printf 'Content-Type: text/event-stream\r\n'
printf 'Cache-Control: no-cache\r\n'
printf 'Connection: close\r\n'
printf '\r\n'
i=1
while [ "$i" -le 5 ]; do
  printf 'data: %s\n\n' "$i"
  sleep 1
  i=$((i+1))
done
```
Need to be careful with `read` in busybox sh from socket; works. Also `Connection: close` causes nc to exit after script ends.

