Potential issue with `docker exec -d` and input: not used.

Potential issue with `spawnSync` env: pass `env: { ...process.env, NO_PROXY: '*', no_proxy: '*' }` for docker? Docker command itself doesn't need proxy for local socket. But Docker Desktop may need? No.

Potential issue: `docker port` output when Docker Desktop maps IPv6: lines. Parse first.

Potential issue: `docker version --format '{{.Server.Version}}'` may return empty if server unavailable; status nonzero.

Potential issue: `docker inspect -f '{{.State.Running}}'` returns "true" or "false".

Potential issue: `dockerExecChecked('command -v nginx ...')` — `command -v netstat` returns path; `netstat` exists. Good.

Potential issue: `readRemotePid` calls docker even when container gone; catches? If docker fails, `dockerExec` returns result with status 127; `readRemotePid` returns null. Fine.

Potential issue: `dockerExec` throws if `result.error` (spawn failure). Fine.

Potential issue: `writeRemoteFile` uses `cat > '${path}'`; if content contains single quotes? Path no; content via stdin no quoting issue. Good.

Potential issue: `serve.sh` script includes `${...}`? In JS template literal, need escape `${`. The serve script has no `${` except maybe `"$line"`; fine. It has `$'...'`? Remove. Use `line=$(printf '%s' "$line" | tr -d '\r')` — inside JS template literal, `\r` would be interpreted by JS as carriage return! We need `\\r` in template string to output `\r` to file. Similarly `\n` in printf: JS template literal `\n` becomes actual newline inside single-quoted shell string, which would break. Must double-escape all backslashes intended for shell.

This is a common pitfall. To avoid, build serve.sh using a normal string array joined by '\n', with String.raw? Use `String.raw` tagged template:
```js
const serveScript = String.raw`#!/bin/sh
...
tr -d '\r'
...
printf 'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n'
...
`
```
`String.raw` preserves backslashes; `${}` still interpolates, but no `${` in script. Great. Use `String.raw`.

Similarly nginx config: `\n` not used except return `"ok"` (no newline). `${SOURCE_PORT}` interpolation works with String.raw? Tagged template still interpolates `${}`; `String.raw` only affects escape sequences. Yes.

For `return 200 "ok";` no issue.

`buildServeScript`:
```js
return String.raw`#!/bin/sh
# ...
while IFS= read -r line; do
  line=$(printf '%s' "$line" | tr -d '\r')
  [ -z "$line" ] && break
done

printf 'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n'
printf 'Content-Type: text/event-stream\r\n'
printf 'Cache-Control: no-cache\r\n'
printf 'Connection: close\r\n'
printf '\r\n'

i=1
while [ "$i" -le 5 ]; do
  printf 'data: %s\n\n' "$i"
  sleep 1
  i=$((i + 1))
done
`
```
In String.raw, `\r\n` stays backslash-r backslash-n, good. `%s\n\n` stays. `$((i + 1))` — `${`? No, `$((` isn't `${`; tagged template doesn't interpolate. Fine.
